  "origins": [
    {
      "id": 28242,
      "title": "Sea Around Us reconstructed marine fisheries catch",
      "description": "Sea Around Us, a research initiative at the University of British Columbia, estimates how much fish and other marine animals the world’s fishers catch each year, from 1950 onward.\n\nMost countries report their catch to the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations (FAO), but these official figures leave out a lot: much of the catch by small-scale, subsistence, and recreational fishers, unreported catch by industrial fleets, and the fish thrown back into the sea. Sea Around Us attempts to reconstruct the full catch of each country by combining the official figures with estimates of these missing parts, drawn from scientific studies, government reports, and local experts.\n\nThe catch is given in tonnes and broken down by fishing country (the country of the fleet doing the fishing), by fishing sector (industrial, artisanal, subsistence, recreational), by type of fishing gear, by what the catch is used for (eaten directly by people, turned into fishmeal and fish oil, mostly for animal feed, or other uses), by whether it was brought to land or thrown back, and by whether it appears in official statistics.\n\nThe catch of marine mammals, reptiles, corals, sponges, and seaweed is not included, nor is fish farming (aquaculture).",
      "producer": "Sea Around Us",
      "citationFull": "Pauly D., Zeller D., Palomares M.L.D. (Editors), 2020. Sea Around Us Concepts, Design and Data (seaaroundus.org).",
